  <info>
    <title>Setting up a CPU shield</title><!-- can be changed via merge
in the assembly -->
    <!-- add author's e-mail -->
    <meta name="maintainer" content="jsindelarova@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract><!-- can be changed via merge in the assembly -->
      <para>
       The general idea is to have one cpuset for the main sensitive workload and a complementary cpuset for the supporting tasks. Resources are distributed top-down, so we must consider all top-level cgroups on the system to ensure proper allocation for the main workload. By default, &systemd; creates the following units: <filename>init.scope</filename>, <filename>system.slice</filename>, <filename>user.slice</filename> and <filename>machine.slice</filename>. You can establish system-wide CPU shields using standard slice drop-in files or dynamic systemctl commands.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <section xml:id="rt-shielding-resources-creating-shield-dropin">
  <title>Setting up systemd CPU shield using drop-in files</title>
   <para>
    This approach isolates standard system processes into non-shielded CPUs while creating a dedicated slice for real-time applications.
    </para>
  <procedure xml:id="pro-setup-cpu-shield">
        <step>
      <para>
        Create drop-in configuration files for <filename>init.scope</filename>, <filename>system.slice</filename>,
        <filename>user.slice</filename>, and <filename>machine.slice</filename> (for example, <filename>40-shielding.conf</filename>) specifying the unshielded CPU cores.
      </para>
      <itemizedlist>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>init.scope</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/init.scope.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Scope]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>system.slice</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/system.slice.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>user.slice</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/user.slice.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>machine.slice</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/machine.slice.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      </itemizedlist> 
    </step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Create a custom workload slice file at <filename>/etc/systemd/system/workload.slice</filename> to assign isolated cores exclusively to real-time tasks.
      </para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/workload.slice</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=2-15</screen>
    </step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Reload the systemd manager configuration to apply the new unit files.
      </para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>systemctl daemon-reload</command></screen>
      <para>
      The command output confirms the shielded range assigned to the slice aka 2-15.
      </para>
    </step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Verify the CPU assignment by checking the <filename>cpuset.cpus</filename> file in the cgroup filesystem.
      </para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /sys/fs/cgroup/workload.slice/cpuset.cpus</command>
2-15</screen>
    </step>
  </procedure>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="rt-shielding-resources-creating-shield-systemctl">
  <title>Setting up systemd CPU shield using <command>systemctl</command></title>
  <para>
  Standard slices can also be restricted on the fly using <command>systemctl set-property</command>. For example, to restrict the operating system to core 0&dash;2:
  </para>
  <itemizedlist>
  <listitem>  
  <para>For the <filename>init.scope</filename>, run:</para>
  <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>systemctl set-property init.scope AllowedCPUs=0-2</command></screen>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
  <para>For the <filename>system.slice</filename>, run:</para>
  <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>systemctl set-property system.slice AllowedCPUs=0-2</command></screen>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
  <para>For the <filename>user.slice</filename>, run:</para>
  <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>systemctl set-property user.slice AllowedCPUs=0-2</command></screen>
  </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
  </section>
</topic>
